## Optional: HTTPS with Caddy (free, auto-SSL)

If you want `https://` instead of `http://` (recommended — Vercel will warn about mixed content):

```bash
# Install Caddy
sudo apt install -y debian-keyring debian-archive-keyring apt-transport-https
curl -1sLf 'https://dl.cloudsmith.io/public/caddy/stable/gpg.key' | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/caddy-stable-archive-keyring.gpg
curl -1sLf 'https://dl.cloudsmith.io/public/caddy/stable/debian.deb.txt' | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/caddy-stable.list
sudo apt update && sudo apt install caddy

# Point a free subdomain to your VM IP
# Option A: Use a free domain from freedns.afraid.org
# Option B: Use nip.io (e.g., <YOUR_IP>.nip.io — no setup needed, but no real SSL)

# Configure Caddy reverse proxy
sudo nano /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
```

```
nexuscore.yourdomain.com {
    reverse_proxy localhost:8000
}
```

```bash
# Open port 443 in Oracle + iptables (same process as port 8000)
sudo systemctl restart caddy
```

Then update your Vercel env var to `https://nexuscore.yourdomain.com`.

---

## Updating After Code Changes

```bash
# On your VM:
cd ~/NexusCore
git pull origin main
cd backend
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt  # only if deps changed
sudo systemctl restart nexuscore

# On Vercel:
# Automatic! Push to GitHub → Vercel redeploys automatically
```

---

## Troubleshooting

| Problem | Fix |
|---|---|
| `Out of Host Capacity` on Oracle | Try a different Availability Domain, or wait and retry |
| `connection refused` on port 8000 | Check both iptables AND Oracle Security List rules |
| Docker `permission denied` | Run `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`, then log out and back in |
| Frontend shows CORS errors | Update `CORS_ORIGINS` in `.env` to include your Vercel URL, restart |
| Mixed content warnings | Set up HTTPS with Caddy (see above) |
| VM reclaimed by Oracle | Keep a small cron job running: `*/5 * * * * curl -s http://localhost:8000/api/v1/health > /dev/null` |
